The Security Council,

Reaffirming the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Recalling all its relevant resolutions on the situation in the Middle East, including the Palestinian question, in particular resolutions 2712 (2023), 2720 (2023), 2728 (2024), and 2735 (2024),

Condemning in the strongest terms the heinous terrorist attacks committed by Hamas and other armed groups on 7 October 2023, including the taking of hostages, killings, and acts of sexual and gender-based violence, and expressing grave outrage at the continued captivity and mistreatment of hostages held in the Gaza Strip for over two years,

Reaffirming the inherent right of all States to individual and collective self-defense in accordance with international law, and reiterating that all parties to the conflict must comply with their obligations under international law, including international humanitarian law and international human rights law, notably regarding the protection of civilians and civilian objects,

Expressing profound concern over the grave humanitarian situation in the Gaza Strip, the dire shortages of essential supplies, and the extreme suffering of Palestinian civilians, and emphasizing the urgent need to expand the safe, unhindered, and sustained delivery of humanitarian assistance at scale,

Commending the continuous and tireless diplomatic efforts of the United States, the Arab Republic of Egypt, and the State of Qatar to secure an agreement for an immediate cessation of hostilities linked to the release of all hostages,

1. Demands the immediate, unconditional, and safe release of all hostages remaining in captivity in Gaza, as well as the immediate return of the remains of deceased hostages;

2. Calls for an immediate and durable ceasefire in Gaza, fully integrated with and conditioned upon the release of all hostages and the implementation of the phased framework outlined in resolution 2735 (2024);

3. Urges all parties to immediately seize the diplomatic framework brokered by the United States, Egypt, and Qatar, and to conclude and implement without delay an agreement providing for a comprehensive cessation of hostilities, the release of hostages, and the rapid surge of humanitarian relief;

4. Demands that all parties facilitate full, rapid, safe, and unhindered humanitarian access throughout the entirety of the Gaza Strip, including through all necessary border crossings, and ensure the safety, security, and freedom of movement of United Nations and humanitarian personnel;

5. Underscores that Hamas must lay down its arms, cease all hostilities and threats against Israel, and can have no role in the future governance, security, or administration of the Gaza Strip, and affirms that Gaza must never again be used as a platform for terrorism;

6. Reaffirms its rejection of any actions that result in demographic or territorial change in the Gaza Strip, including any reduction of its territory or forced displacement of the civilian population;

7. Reiterates its unwavering commitment to the vision of the two-State solution, where two democratic States, Israel and Palestine, live side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders, consistent with international law and relevant United Nations resolutions, and stresses in this regard the vital importance of reunifying the Gaza Strip and the West Bank under a revitalized and reformed Palestinian Authority;

8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

